# Vulnerability details

## Impact
After an ownership transfer of the protocol , the `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` role will be empty.Protocol cannot function properly without  `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE`  cause this is the single most important role in the protocol . Functionalities that only `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` can call cannot be executed anymore ! 
## Proof of Concept
The `_grantRole` function is  overridden from `_grantRole` function of `AccessControl.sol` . If given role is   `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` then it revokes the `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE` and sets the new `account` as the  `_currentDefaultAdmin `  . But it never grants the `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE`  to the `account ` in the inheriting `AccessControl.sol` contract . This leaves the  `DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE `  empty ! This will brick the whole protocol in case of an event of ownership transfer cause it is the single most imprtant role in the protocol to manage different scenarios .
```solidity 
 function _grantRole(bytes32 role, address account) internal override {
    if (role == DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE) {
