# Vulnerability details

## Impact

An attacker can exploit a reentrancy vulnerability in the `transferInRewards` function of the StakedUSDe contract to steal funds from the contract. the `transferInRewards` does not check for reentrancy before transferring assets.

## Proof of Concept

The `transferInRewards` function in StakedUSDe.sol does not check for reentrancy before transferring assets from the rewarder to the contract. It is possible for an attacker to call the `transferInRewards` function and trigger reentrancy. This means that an attacker could call the `transferInRewards` function multiple times, transferring the same assets to the contract multiple times.

**[StakedUSDe.sol#transferInRewards](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ethena/blob/ee67d9b542642c9757a6b826c82d0cae60256509/contracts/StakedUSDe.sol#L89-L99)**

```solidity
function transferInRewards(uint256 amount) external nonReentrant onlyRole(REWARDER_ROLE) notZero(amount) {

  // transferred assets from rewarder before reentrancy check
  IERC20(asset()).safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);

  // emit event and update state
  emit RewardsReceived(amount, newVestingAmount);

}
```

This allows an attacker to call `transferInRewards` multiple times, transferring in the same assets repeatedly, before calling `withdraw` to drain the duplicate funds.

## Attack Path

1. Attacker calls `transferInRewards` with a large amount
2. Contract transfers assets from rewarder to itself 
3. Attacker calls `withdraw` to drain funds before transfer completes
4. Attacker repeats steps 1-3 to drain more funds 

