# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The `verifyNonce` function in the USDe protocol contract contains a vulnerability that allows an attacker with the `MINTER_ROLE` or `REDEEMER_ROLE` to mint or redeem USDe using an invalid nonce. This effectively allows unauthorized minting or redemption of USDe without requiring any collateral to be deposited. This could allow an attacker to steal USDe tokens from the protocol or manipulate the market.

## Proof of Concept 

The `verifyNonce` function only checks if the invalidator bit is set for a given nonce: **[EthenaMinting.sol#verifyNonce](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-ethena/blob/ee67d9b542642c9757a6b826c82d0cae60256509/contracts/EthenaMinting.sol#L377-L386)**

```solidity
function verifyNonce(address sender, uint256 nonce) public view override returns (bool, uint256, uint256, uint256) {

  // ...

  uint256 invalidator = invalidatorStorage[invalidatorSlot];

  if (invalidator & invalidatorBit != 0) revert InvalidNonce();

  return (true, invalidatorSlot, invalidator, invalidatorBit);

}
```

An attacker can exploit this by:

1. Calling `mint` or `redeem` with a valid nonce. This will set the invalidator bit.

2. Calling `mint` or `redeem` again with the same nonce. `verifyNonce` will return true even though the nonce has been used.

This allows the attacker to mint or redeem multiple times with the same nonce.

